1
00:00:02,790 --> 00:00:07,830
This is Software Engineering Radio, the podcast for professional developers on the web at SE-Radio.net.

2
00:00:07,840 --> 00:00:19,070
SE Radio is brought to you by the IEEE Computer Society and by IEEE Software Magazine online at computer.org/software.

3
00:00:20,550 --> 00:00:23,870
Hello and welcome to Software Engineering Radio. This is your host Arun Ponnusamy.

4
00:00:23,880 --> 00:00:30,510
Today I'm joined by Will Wilson, CEO and co-founder of Antithesis.

5
00:00:30,510 --> 00:00:36,510
Will started off in biotech and went on to work on distributed systems at FoundationDB, Apple, and Google.

6
00:00:36,520 --> 00:00:44,550
And then he eventually went on to found Antithesis, an autonomous testing platform based on deterministic simulation testing.

7
00:00:44,560 --> 00:00:47,510
Will, welcome to the show. Thank you so much for having me.

8
00:00:47,520 --> 00:00:55,150
For our listeners who may not be familiar with the concept, could you share what is DST or deterministic simulation testing?

9
00:00:55,160 --> 00:00:57,350
Yeah, sure.

10
00:00:57,350 --> 00:01:00,470
So, let's start with the way that people normally test software, right?

11
00:01:00,470 --> 00:01:10,710
So, the way they normally test software is first you write some code, and then you try and think about what situations you want to cover with a test, what situations you want to make sure are going to work in real life,

12
00:01:10,710 --> 00:01:14,350
and then you write some tests to cover those situations, right?

13
00:01:14,350 --> 00:01:21,630
And then you occasionally run those tests, maybe you do them on every CI build, or maybe you do them on every PR, maybe you do them only when you're about to release your software,

14
00:01:21,640 --> 00:01:29,110
but they cover those cases, and you hope that when you deploy to production, everything is going to go okay.

15
00:01:29,110 --> 00:01:31,630
Now, let me ask you a question.

16
00:01:31,630 --> 00:01:34,870
Do you believe if all your tests pass, that everything is going to be okay?

17
00:01:34,880 --> 00:01:36,830
I would like to think so.

18
00:01:36,840 --> 00:01:38,750
But do you actually?

19
00:01:38,760 --> 00:01:44,951
Uh well, if anything last 20 years have shown me, no. Right, right. Exactly, so why is that?

20
00:01:44,960 --> 00:01:53,870
Well, it's because we, I just said, you're writing the tests that cover the cases that you thought were important to cover.

21
00:01:53,870 --> 00:01:59,270
But the things that are actually going to screw you in production are the situations that you did not think of ahead of time.

22
00:01:59,270 --> 00:02:01,390
They're the things you did not think to cover, right?

23
00:02:01,390 --> 00:02:07,390
And so what that means is this conventional strategy to writing tests is very very good for catching regressions, right?

24
00:02:07,390 --> 00:02:16,230
If there's something that I know my software does correctly and I accidentally break it in a new version, if I have a test for it, that's a pretty good at catching that.

25
00:02:16,230 --> 00:02:27,030
But a test is very bad at finding out if my software actually works in the real world in all the unanticipated or unexpected situations that the real world will throw at me.

26
00:02:27,040 --> 00:02:38,390
So, deterministic simulation testing is a very different strategy, which tries to actually give us the confidence that if our test pass, things will go well in the real world.

27
00:02:38,400 --> 00:02:43,790
And the way it does that is basically by constructing a model of the real world,

28
00:02:43,800 --> 00:02:58,230
a whole system simulation where random requests come in and ask your software to do things and tasks are going to run in random orders and these requests are going to have slightly randomized parameters.

29
00:02:58,230 --> 00:03:04,030
And also the things that happen in the real world that can go wrong are also going to happen, right?

30
00:03:04,030 --> 00:03:05,750
So, occasionally requests will fail.

31
00:03:05,750 --> 00:03:07,750
Requests to downstream dependent systems will fail.

32
00:03:07,750 --> 00:03:09,750
Network requests may get delayed or reordered.

33
00:03:09,760 --> 00:03:14,110
Threads may randomly pause and run later.

34
00:03:14,110 --> 00:03:18,350
The garbage collector may just turn on and that whole node may slow down, right?

35
00:03:18,350 --> 00:03:25,310
All the stuff that causes people to get paged in the middle of the night and causes weird production behaviors that nobody can understand, we're going to do all that, too.

36
00:03:25,310 --> 00:03:37,949
And we're going to do it at a vastly accelerated rate so that we cover many many years or centuries worth of real world badness in just a few seconds or minutes in our tests.

37
00:03:37,949 --> 00:03:47,830
And if our software survives all that and things actually go okay, we're now going to have the confidence to actually deploy and to actually feel like things will be all right when we have it in the real world.

38
00:03:47,840 --> 00:03:57,710
The final ingredient to this, the part that makes it deterministic is our model of the real world,

39
00:03:57,710 --> 00:04:03,070
unlike the real world, is perfectly deterministic.

40
00:04:03,070 --> 00:04:07,670
Meaning it's all driven from a random number generator that's under our control, right?

41
00:04:07,670 --> 00:04:19,110
So, this is very important because sometimes what happens is there's a particular Let's say that a packet takes 21 milliseconds to get from service A to service B.

42
00:04:19,110 --> 00:04:22,270
And if it takes 19 milliseconds instead, something goes wrong, right?

43
00:04:22,270 --> 00:04:23,790
There's some distributed race condition.

44
00:04:23,790 --> 00:04:27,150
Something something loses and as a result there's some bug that a user can see.

45
00:04:27,160 --> 00:04:30,710
You may have great difficulty replicating that situation again, right?

46
00:04:30,720 --> 00:04:35,750
Maybe it only happened it only took that long because of some very rare set of circumstances.

47
00:04:35,760 --> 00:04:45,110
But the determinism of DST means that any set of circumstances, no matter how rare, no matter how unusual, we can perfectly replicate.

48
00:04:45,110 --> 00:04:51,750
And so if we see a bug once, we can see it as many times as we want and we can debug it with very very high efficiency.

49
00:04:51,760 --> 00:04:56,230
How you actually achieve that is sort of the hard part.

50
00:04:56,230 --> 00:04:58,710
But once you have it, it's an incredible asset.

51
00:04:58,720 --> 00:05:00,470
Awesome, thank you.

52
00:05:00,470 --> 00:05:05,390
Before I dive in, I would like to get some of the origin stories here.

53
00:05:05,390 --> 00:05:08,310
I hear it started off in FoundationDB. Could you share some of that background there?

54
00:05:08,320 --> 00:05:12,550
Sure, yeah. So, this is sort of a fun story.

55
00:05:12,550 --> 00:05:19,230
Basically, actually even before FoundationDB, my co-founder Dave Scherer, before FoundationDB, had another company called Visual Sciences,

56
00:05:19,240 --> 00:05:31,790
which you can think of as having made a very very early web analytics product that was backed by a very powerful analytic database that's sort of a very early version of Google BigQuery, right?

57
00:05:31,800 --> 00:05:36,870
And this would have been in the early 2000s. So, it was really very very ahead of its time.

58
00:05:36,880 --> 00:05:41,310
So, when Dave was building that big analytic data store,

59
00:05:41,310 --> 00:05:52,510
he was really interested in using model-based techniques to look for sort of long-tail performance pathologies and like strange sort of high latency corner cases.

60
00:05:52,520 --> 00:05:59,830
And so he built this little like model based simulation of the database to see if he could find these issues.

61
00:05:59,840 --> 00:06:06,790
It's sort of like in some ways similar to formal methods if you're familiar with that, but he was using it to analyze performance rather than correctness.

62
00:06:06,800 --> 00:06:16,470
The problem that Dave ran into was of course once he had this all working in the model how do you know that your real code is behaving anything like the model, right?

63
00:06:16,480 --> 00:06:22,830
And so that company got sold and and that never went anywhere and but it stuck around in the back of his mind.

64
00:06:22,840 --> 00:06:31,470
And so then when we were doing FoundationDB he had the same idea again this time to use it to study correctness rather than performance.

65
00:06:31,480 --> 00:06:39,710
But then he had the further idea what if we just made the actual program into the model, right?

66
00:06:39,710 --> 00:06:49,790
Then you would never have to worry that oh I did some analysis on this model and it gave me this result, but actually my real world code has a bug in it so it doesn't perfectly match that specification.

67
00:06:49,800 --> 00:07:00,030
So the trouble is you know real world code has things like concurrency and it has things like it reads the clock and it gets random numbers from the operating system and it it does all kinds of things

68
00:07:00,030 --> 00:07:09,830
that make it fundamentally non-deterministic and make it so that if you run some battery of tests or conduct some simulation the result might vary from time to time.

69
00:07:09,840 --> 00:07:20,350
And so we set about writing FoundationDB in a very special way so that it could be perfectly simulated.

70
00:07:20,350 --> 00:07:30,990
We did things like we used our own user space concurrency primitives and scheduling so that the kernel was never deciding which task to run.

71
00:07:30,990 --> 00:07:36,310
It's like sort of similar to the Node.js concurrency model, but we were doing that in C++ long beforehand.

72
00:07:36,320 --> 00:07:45,470
We obviously had to mock out or stub out any form of network communication between nodes and handle that in process.

73
00:07:45,470 --> 00:07:51,710
We had a fake clock and a fake random number generator that got accessed instead of the ones provided by the OS.

74
00:07:51,710 --> 00:07:55,070
And as we sort of knocked away each of these forms of randomness and each of these forms of non-determinism,

75
00:07:55,080 --> 00:08:08,030
the simulation became more controllable until we eventually got to a point where it really was like you could really just run a program and it would simulate an entire interacting FoundationDB cluster

76
00:08:08,040 --> 00:08:22,030
with possibly dozens or hundreds of simulated nodes speaking over simulated networks with, you know, randomly introduced networking delays and congestion behavior and whatever else you want.

77
00:08:22,030 --> 00:08:27,830
And this was just such an insanely productive mode in which to work.

78
00:08:27,830 --> 00:08:34,029
Like people who are familiar with FoundationDB these days mostly know it for its reputation,

79
00:08:34,029 --> 00:08:40,469
which is being one of the highest quality databases ever made, never losing your data, never doing anything wrong.

80
00:08:40,469 --> 00:08:42,070
And it's true, we got that.

81
00:08:42,070 --> 00:08:44,790
And we got that because of this technique.

82
00:08:44,790 --> 00:08:50,230
But the thing that made almost an even bigger difference was that we were able to get that with a very, very small team.

83
00:08:50,240 --> 00:09:04,510
Because what this technique enables is like this incredible form of pair programming with the computer where you write some code and then you run some simulations and you say, "Did I introduce any bugs?"

84
00:09:04,510 --> 00:09:09,030
And, you know, if the answer is yes, then like you just try again.

85
00:09:09,030 --> 00:09:12,110
It's like having an oracle or a genie that can tell you if your code is correct, right?

86
00:09:12,110 --> 00:09:16,710
It's like this incredible, incredible accelerator for an entire team.

87
00:09:16,710 --> 00:09:23,310
And that was what made us really excited about this technique and I think this is why so many others have started adopting it and picking it up as well.

88
00:09:23,320 --> 00:09:26,630
Interesting. That reminds me as you go deeper, right?

89
00:09:26,640 --> 00:09:30,550
I mean to really simulation, you need a model.

90
00:09:30,560 --> 00:09:35,430
So, how do you avoid the paradox of hey look, I don't know what I don't know and I build this model?

91
00:09:35,440 --> 00:09:40,030
Yeah, that is a really, really good question.

92
00:09:40,030 --> 00:09:45,590
And basically, the answer is you have to err a little bit on the side of your model being worse than reality, rather than better than reality, right?

93
00:09:45,600 --> 00:09:47,950
Could you give an example of that, what worse looks like?

94
00:09:47,960 --> 00:09:51,270
Yeah, sure.

95
00:09:51,270 --> 00:09:59,750
So, your network in your data center is occasionally going to have periods of being down, and it's going to occasionally have periods where it's a little bit slow,

96
00:09:59,750 --> 00:10:03,030
and it's occasionally going to have periods where it's like working great.

97
00:10:03,030 --> 00:10:11,510
And these periods in your network are probably totally uncorrelated with what your program is doing, right?

98
00:10:11,510 --> 00:10:18,590
And so, suppose that there's some bug, and the bug only happens if your program is in the middle of some particular operation, right?

99
00:10:18,600 --> 00:10:29,390
Like you you're doing some I don't know, you're doing some transaction on your database, and at exactly that moment, the network does something a little bit wrong, right?

100
00:10:29,390 --> 00:10:31,070
Or it's a little bit slower than you expect, or whatever.

101
00:10:31,070 --> 00:10:34,510
And only if these two things happen at the same time, this bug happens.

102
00:10:34,510 --> 00:10:36,830
And let's say the bug is really, really bad.

103
00:10:36,840 --> 00:10:48,990
In order to make that bug happen very reliably and very reproducibly in our tests, we don't want to make the network completely uncorrelated from what your program is doing.

104
00:10:48,990 --> 00:10:54,110
We want to make it basically as malicious as it can be, right?

105
00:10:54,110 --> 00:10:59,310
We want to try making the network bad at each different sort of moment that your program could be doing something,

106
00:10:59,320 --> 00:11:06,870
and we want to like artificially increase the probability that this network badness happens at exactly the right spot to trigger that bug.

107
00:11:06,870 --> 00:11:16,910
Because that way you see it in your test like in a few seconds or a few minutes, rather than having to wait for days or weeks, which you would have to do if it were happening in production.

108
00:11:16,910 --> 00:11:23,750
And so, that's kind of an example of where if you make the simulation more hostile than reality,

109
00:11:23,750 --> 00:11:31,310
it can catch stuff quicker than it would happen in the real world, and also ensures that you don't miss things that That happen in the real world.

110
00:11:31,320 --> 00:11:33,830
Mhm.

111
00:11:33,830 --> 00:11:40,750
So, what would be a good I suppose evaluation or identification function to know when or what a bad network is.

112
00:11:40,750 --> 00:11:46,710
For example, a network loss of 10% may be really good in some applications, but really bad in some.

113
00:11:46,710 --> 00:11:50,470
So, how do you decide That's totally true.

114
00:11:50,470 --> 00:11:59,710
And furthermore, there can be a very dangerous pattern, where if you basically make these parameters a like a little bit wrong, right?

115
00:11:59,710 --> 00:12:03,070
You could accidentally get yourself in a situation where the simulation isn't really testing anything.

116
00:12:03,080 --> 00:12:05,030
Right?

117
00:12:05,030 --> 00:12:08,390
Consider the corner case where we just make it so the network never transports anything at all.

118
00:12:08,390 --> 00:12:10,430
Now, your program never does anything.

119
00:12:10,430 --> 00:12:14,470
And then all your tests are green because we didn't find any bugs because your program didn't do anything.

120
00:12:14,470 --> 00:12:16,670
That would be a bad outcome, right?

121
00:12:16,680 --> 00:12:26,230
And so, there's a few techniques that we can use to to avoid this and to make the simulation really good and really well-tuned instead.

122
00:12:26,230 --> 00:12:34,550
One is just let's look at things like code coverage, or the more sophisticated version of this is like behavioral coverage, right?

123
00:12:34,550 --> 00:12:44,630
If you have certain corner cases in your code that you know are interesting and are important to cover, you should just log a little message when that situation happens.

124
00:12:44,630 --> 00:12:50,270
And then at the end of a test run, we should make an assertion that we're able to see all those messages, right?

125
00:12:50,270 --> 00:12:52,550
That we basically got at some point to every one of those situations.

126
00:12:52,560 --> 00:12:58,390
And the generalization of that is something that we call sometimes assertions.

127
00:12:58,390 --> 00:13:00,470
They're like the dual or the opposite of normal assertions, right?

128
00:13:00,480 --> 00:13:10,990
Rather than asserting that something is always or never happening, we're asserting that it sometimes happens because that's an important indication about whether our test is working effectively.

129
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:14,310
Is that in a probabilistic model or is it truly sometimes?

130
00:13:14,320 --> 00:13:17,870
Right, right. So, it's like at least one, right?

131
00:13:17,870 --> 00:13:23,870
We're saying that if I run my program a thousand times, I want to guarantee that I see this behavior at least once.

132
00:13:23,870 --> 00:13:26,150
That is a property of my testing system, right?

133
00:13:26,160 --> 00:13:30,070
It's like I should be able to make the situation occur.

134
00:13:30,070 --> 00:13:33,390
Like the ultimate guarantor though is just like are you still seeing bugs in production?

135
00:13:33,400 --> 00:13:42,270
Basically, I think people regardless of whether doing DST or not, if you ever see a bug in production that is actually two bugs.

136
00:13:42,280 --> 00:13:48,390
It's a bug in your program and it's also a bug in your tests and you should maybe not solve them in that order.

137
00:13:48,390 --> 00:13:50,190
Maybe you should solve the bug in your test first.

138
00:13:50,200 --> 00:13:52,030
Who knows what else it will turn up.

139
00:13:52,040 --> 00:14:01,750
And this is like a very This is a a practice that requires a little bit of discipline to get into, but once you do, it's it's so incredibly powerful.

140
00:14:01,750 --> 00:14:10,230
Just like understand why it is that you didn't see that bug the minute it was introduced.

141
00:14:10,230 --> 00:14:18,470
And often it will point to an entire area of your of your software or of your organization or whatever that is not working correctly.

142
00:14:18,480 --> 00:14:21,110
Mhm.

143
00:14:21,110 --> 00:14:25,030
So it it sounded like there was a bootstrap phase here for a team to kind of onboard.

144
00:14:25,030 --> 00:14:28,630
Let's let's say I'm writing a game, right? A multiplayer game running on the internet.

145
00:14:28,630 --> 00:14:30,270
It's probably as low level as it gets, right?

146
00:14:30,280 --> 00:14:33,910
What would the bootstrap phase for me be to adopt this?

147
00:14:33,920 --> 00:14:38,190
Right, so this gets a little bit into like sort of what our company does.

148
00:14:38,200 --> 00:14:44,910
Basically, up until we came around, this technique was very very powerful,

149
00:14:44,920 --> 00:14:59,270
but it was a giant pain to adopt because it required you to do things like mock out your dependencies and somehow take control of like concurrency and scheduling within your program, simulate all of your network connections, right?

150
00:14:59,280 --> 00:15:04,630
There there's sort of a lot that had to be done.

151
00:15:04,630 --> 00:15:13,270
And there were applications and use cases where reliability and correctness was so important that people would do all these things.

152
00:15:13,270 --> 00:15:18,310
Like I I know of actually quite a large number of people now cuz they they come talk to me, right?

153
00:15:18,310 --> 00:15:23,510
Who actually started doing this in the years after FoundationDB got acquired by Apple.

154
00:15:23,510 --> 00:15:28,270
The team at Dropbox that worked on their synchronization technology built their own DST.

155
00:15:28,270 --> 00:15:32,070
Tiger Beetle is a very well-known financial transactions database.

156
00:15:32,070 --> 00:15:34,191
They built their own DST. It's It's quite good.

157
00:15:34,200 --> 00:15:49,190
You know, a number of people actually in the finance or fintech or or crypto space built their own DST and did very good job at it because they really wanted to catch any kind of problem before it hit prod because it could result in losing tons of money.

158
00:15:49,190 --> 00:15:57,630
And obviously other database vendors were also doing this as were a number of teams that got influenced by what we had done at FoundationDB.

159
00:15:57,630 --> 00:16:05,190
And by the way, after I went to work at Google, I learned that there were teams inside Google that had done this and just never told anybody about it.

160
00:16:05,190 --> 00:16:09,310
So, I think this is a technique that's been independently reinvented a few times.

161
00:16:09,320 --> 00:16:17,230
But, it remained a thing that only very very committed people

162
00:16:17,240 --> 00:16:25,070
who really felt like their program had to be perfectly reliable would do because the adoption costs were high.

163
00:16:25,080 --> 00:16:39,590
So, our new company, Antithesis, tries to drastically reduce the adoption cost for this style of testing by basically doing like solving two of the problems that you and I have already discussed.

164
00:16:39,600 --> 00:16:46,750
One is how do you take an arbitrary collection of software and make it deterministic, right?

165
00:16:46,750 --> 00:16:50,790
And prior to to us being around, that took a lot of work.

166
00:16:50,790 --> 00:16:54,790
And what we've done is write a hypervisor, which is deterministic.

167
00:16:54,790 --> 00:17:00,630
And you can take an arbitrary collection of software and just put it in the hypervisor, and now it's deterministic, too.

168
00:17:00,640 --> 00:17:05,189
And so, that already on its own makes this much easier.

169
00:17:05,199 --> 00:17:10,550
The second problem is the one you touched on, which is what's the right network drop rate, right?

170
00:17:10,550 --> 00:17:16,069
What is the right way of tuning all these parameters of the simulation to find all the bugs and to find those quickly as possible.

171
00:17:16,079 --> 00:17:23,390
This is like a very hard optimization machine learning science problem.

172
00:17:23,390 --> 00:17:30,550
And it's sort of unreasonable in my opinion for every team on earth to try and solve this independently.

173
00:17:30,550 --> 00:17:37,830
And so we have also just done a huge amount of research and a huge amount of development

174
00:17:37,830 --> 00:17:47,670
to make this simulation like self-tuning and to make it very intelligent and very good at finding the correct parameters and the correct settings. So you just don't even have to think about that.

175
00:17:47,680 --> 00:17:59,590
And so the goal here is to make it much much much easier for people to adopt this stuff which hopefully just makes them a lot faster at writing software and also produce much higher quality software.

176
00:17:59,600 --> 00:18:02,510
I want to quickly touch back on a point you mentioned about the cost, right?

177
00:18:02,520 --> 00:18:09,150
What would the cost for these organizations to adopt DST in their own setting engineering wise, financially?

178
00:18:09,160 --> 00:18:18,751
Yeah, I think if you don't have a solution like antithesis, then the the question is really dominated by your dependencies. Right?

179
00:18:18,751 --> 00:18:27,710
Because the hard thing about DST is that unless you have a hypervisor, you need to somehow make it so all your dependencies are deterministic too.

180
00:18:27,720 --> 00:18:35,430
And that like really does just hugely limit what you can do.

181
00:18:35,430 --> 00:18:37,030
Most software in the world is not deterministic, right?

182
00:18:37,030 --> 00:18:43,270
It's got concurrency, it's got threads, it it checks timers, it does all kinds of stuff.

183
00:18:43,270 --> 00:18:56,430
When we were developing FoundationDB, we were so influenced by this DST style of development that we actually started removing our dependencies to make it easier to test in this way.

184
00:18:56,430 --> 00:19:03,550
So very early versions of our database were using Apache Zookeeper for coordination for instance.

185
00:19:03,550 --> 00:19:07,670
And that just became a thing that we could no longer accept because it made it harder to simulate.

186
00:19:07,680 --> 00:19:15,430
And so we deleted Zookeeper and wrote our own Paxos implementation that could be simulated and that made sense for us.

187
00:19:15,440 --> 00:19:27,830
But that's a very, very hard thing to ask somebody to do if they're just starting to adopt this technology, which is one reason that I think that thus far it's only been it's only happened in relatively specialized places.

188
00:19:27,840 --> 00:19:32,270
It's almost like asking them to re-reinvent or re-implement what you what you guys are doing.

189
00:19:32,280 --> 00:19:34,670
Yeah, that's right.

190
00:19:34,670 --> 00:19:37,790
It's it's just it's just a very high barrier to entry and that's what we're trying to fix.

191
00:19:37,800 --> 00:19:41,270
Fair enough. That brings me back to the hypervisor that you have mentioned.

192
00:19:41,270 --> 00:19:43,110
I mean, can you tell me more about that?

193
00:19:43,120 --> 00:19:44,950
Sure.

194
00:19:44,950 --> 00:19:48,950
So we started from the question of how could we make DST easy to adopt?

195
00:19:48,950 --> 00:19:54,550
And it's like, well, we need to make it so that everybody can make their program deterministic super easily.

196
00:19:54,550 --> 00:20:01,630
And then it's like, well you know, if your program is written in Java, you could try and make a deterministic JVM.

197
00:20:01,630 --> 00:20:07,631
And if your program's written in C, I guess you could try and make a deterministic Linux user space, right? Actually, Facebook did that.

198
00:20:07,631 --> 00:20:09,150
That project's called Hermit.

199
00:20:09,160 --> 00:20:12,790
But we're going to have customers who use every language.

200
00:20:12,790 --> 00:20:15,350
So we can't just like play whack-a-mole here doing all these things.

201
00:20:15,360 --> 00:20:24,190
And then it's like, well, so much of the value is being able to test microservices where service A is talking to service B over a network.

202
00:20:24,190 --> 00:20:26,270
But now we need to make that networking deterministic.

203
00:20:26,270 --> 00:20:28,670
How are we going to do that?

204
00:20:28,670 --> 00:20:35,310
Like, oh wow, we need some crazy networking library that we can drop in, but everybody does networking differently.

205
00:20:35,310 --> 00:20:45,190
How do we Right, like so very, very quickly you get to like, well I guess we should try and get under all of these things and make a deterministic operating system.

206
00:20:45,200 --> 00:20:46,950
But then you hit another problem.

207
00:20:46,950 --> 00:20:54,230
Well, first of all, not everybody in the world uses Linux and we would love to be able to have customers on Windows someday and and so on.

208
00:20:54,230 --> 00:21:01,790
But also the interface between the Linux kernel and everything running on top of it is really complicated.

209
00:21:01,790 --> 00:21:03,751
And it's always changing. Right?

210
00:21:03,760 --> 00:21:12,710
And so maintaining a perfect deterministic Linux as a small company just seemed like a very tall order.

211
00:21:12,720 --> 00:21:14,670
And then we were like, you know what?

212
00:21:14,680 --> 00:21:21,110
You know what is a very simple interface that has not changed in 40 50 years?

213
00:21:21,120 --> 00:21:24,350
The x86 CPU instructions.

214
00:21:24,350 --> 00:21:30,830
Like look, so let's just get under the operating system and let's let's emulate a deterministic computer.

215
00:21:30,840 --> 00:21:42,230
Where no matter what the operating system does, no matter what interrupts it sets, no matter how it, you know, is managing memory or doing anything from the outside,

216
00:21:42,230 --> 00:21:49,350
or rather from the inside, it it's like living in a perfect isolated hermetic deterministic universe and it it can't actually cause any non-determinism.

217
00:21:49,360 --> 00:21:53,590
And then once you build that it's like so easy to adopt.

218
00:21:53,590 --> 00:21:57,070
Now people don't have to change their software at all.

219
00:21:57,070 --> 00:22:03,270
They just give it to us, we run it inside the magic box, and it's perfectly deterministic. So it's it's really very very powerful.

220
00:22:03,280 --> 00:22:13,750
How does this overcome the I mean this box is still in isolation or is external to your TCP stack itself or or the network layer itself? So how do you reconcile that?

221
00:22:13,760 --> 00:22:15,910
Right.

222
00:22:15,910 --> 00:22:20,750
So basically what we do is we take our customers' multiple microservices, right?

223
00:22:20,750 --> 00:22:24,790
Representing a bunch of different physical nodes in their in their compute back end.

224
00:22:24,800 --> 00:22:26,790
Plus whatever their dependencies are.

225
00:22:26,800 --> 00:22:31,270
Maybe they have a database or maybe they've got some API server or whatever.

226
00:22:31,280 --> 00:22:38,630
And we take them all and we put them inside the same guest in operating system environment, which is Linux.

227
00:22:38,630 --> 00:22:44,671
And then we connect them over TCP locally inside the VM. Right?

228
00:22:44,671 --> 00:22:50,750
So they can speak over network connections between different Docker containers running all in the same version of Linux.

229
00:22:50,760 --> 00:22:55,470
And then we can actually reach in and interfere with those network connections, right?

230
00:22:55,470 --> 00:22:59,990
We can slow the packets, we can drop packets, we can we can do whatever.

231
00:22:59,990 --> 00:23:05,031
We can create simulated partitions, we can and we can correlate all this with the code that's running, right? The thing I told you before.

232
00:23:05,040 --> 00:23:09,390
Because we see what code is running cuz we control the operating system.

233
00:23:09,390 --> 00:23:12,270
And so it's actually it's actually a very complete solution.

234
00:23:12,280 --> 00:23:14,510
It works very well.

235
00:23:14,510 --> 00:23:24,670
The one thing that's a little bit of friction is if you are highly dependent on some third-party web service that you cannot put in a container and have a hard time mocking.

236
00:23:24,680 --> 00:23:26,830
That is going to cause a problem.

237
00:23:26,830 --> 00:23:35,630
So we try and reduce that friction for our customers also by writing very good, very high-quality mocks for a lot of common web services.

238
00:23:35,630 --> 00:23:40,710
So for example, we have a whole fake AWS that we can like run inside the simulation with you.

239
00:23:40,710 --> 00:23:46,470
And if your program uses AWS, it just runs in there and uses the fake one and can't even tell the difference.

240
00:23:46,470 --> 00:23:50,110
We actually test our own stuff that way cuz we're very heavy AWS users.

241
00:23:50,120 --> 00:23:53,710
We can do the same for Snowflake and GCP and Stripe and you know, whatever.

242
00:23:53,710 --> 00:23:55,670
We can write a good mock for it.

243
00:23:55,670 --> 00:24:02,790
And that's that's nice because basically just a few of those covers the vast majority of APIs that people use.

244
00:24:02,800 --> 00:24:06,990
And if there's a long tail, you know, I think we'll we'll get to it someday.

245
00:24:07,000 --> 00:24:21,630
The other thing that the hypervisor gives us, which is very, very powerful, is extremely efficient and rapid snapshotting and restore of the whole state of the guest operating system.

246
00:24:21,640 --> 00:24:23,510
Right?

247
00:24:23,510 --> 00:24:29,510
So if you use a VMware hypervisor, for instance, you can like snapshot that VM and then you can reload it, right?

248
00:24:29,510 --> 00:24:31,350
And that that takes a little bit of time, but you can do it.

249
00:24:31,350 --> 00:24:38,030
Our hypervisor is optimized to perform that operation incredibly quickly and efficiently.

250
00:24:38,040 --> 00:24:47,510
More than that even, when it's running multiple VMs simultaneously on the same computer,

251
00:24:47,510 --> 00:24:55,351
those VMs can actually address the same physical memory if their local version of that memory hasn't changed. Right?

252
00:24:55,351 --> 00:25:02,510
So we're basically doing copy-on-write on all of the memory of the guest operating system.

253
00:25:02,510 --> 00:25:06,510
So, now if we run 100 VMs in parallel on the same machine, they're not using 100 times the memory.

254
00:25:06,510 --> 00:25:14,350
We're basically like deduplicating all of the memory between all the different VMs, which is a thing that I don't know anybody else's hypervisor can do that.

255
00:25:14,360 --> 00:25:15,950
I'm sure there is one somewhere.

256
00:25:15,950 --> 00:25:19,550
But, what it means is that we can now test your stuff really efficiently, right?

257
00:25:19,560 --> 00:25:30,990
Cuz we can use huge amounts of parallelism without drastically increasing the memory requirements, which just at the end of the day means that we can find bugs faster, and we can find rare bugs faster.

258
00:25:31,000 --> 00:25:38,630
It's interesting because the implication of this means that a, you kind of running the entire customer world in a single sandbox.

259
00:25:38,640 --> 00:25:47,470
And you can also do things like simulate connection between A and B if it was in the same zone or same region or even across regions.

260
00:25:47,480 --> 00:25:53,070
Because you want to know that, hey, look, this is for DR and for various kinds of uh a fault tolerance, right?

261
00:25:53,080 --> 00:25:59,350
That's exactly right. All that we have to do is really change the latency, right? On the virtual connection. Or we And error rates.

262
00:25:59,360 --> 00:26:02,430
And error rates, that's right. And also, you know, correlated failures, right?

263
00:26:02,440 --> 00:26:08,390
We'll make things in the same DR region fail together more often, but not across regions.

264
00:26:08,390 --> 00:26:11,430
And people use us to test DR all the time.

265
00:26:11,430 --> 00:26:14,270
People also love using us to test live upgrades.

266
00:26:14,270 --> 00:26:16,630
Cuz that's a very scary situation for many teams.

267
00:26:16,630 --> 00:26:20,550
You have some big service deployed, and you have customers who are using it, right?

268
00:26:20,550 --> 00:26:24,590
And you want to incrementally upgrade your cluster.

269
00:26:24,590 --> 00:26:31,430
And while the upgrade is rolling out, you're going to have servers with both the old version and the new version serving requests simultaneously.

270
00:26:31,440 --> 00:26:33,470
Does that all work?

271
00:26:33,470 --> 00:26:35,670
It can be very hard to test that.

272
00:26:35,670 --> 00:26:37,470
But, it's quite easy to test it with our environment.

273
00:26:37,480 --> 00:26:39,830
Interesting.

