00:27:59,951 --> 00:28:01,550
What is the workflow for them?

292
00:28:01,560 --> 00:28:03,430
Yeah, that's a great question.

293
00:28:03,430 --> 00:28:09,510
So I think there's two main ways that people do this.

294
00:28:09,510 --> 00:28:16,910
And one of our long-term product goals, actually not even long-term, one of our relatively short-term now product goals is to bridge these two ways of using it.

295
00:28:16,910 --> 00:28:27,270
So the first way people use this is sort of offline as a very intensive check phase, maybe overnight, right?

296
00:28:27,270 --> 00:28:32,710
Or maybe like they do tons and tons of testing right before release, before rolling out a release, right?

297
00:28:32,710 --> 00:28:38,670
So the idea here is like you spend all day writing code and then whatever the last version of your code is,

298
00:28:38,670 --> 00:28:47,631
the default branch, you send that to us when people leave work and then we test it all night and then in the morning you come in and you see what your results are. And that can be cool.

299
00:28:47,640 --> 00:28:53,070
It does mean that we can find pretty rare, pretty hard-to-find bugs because we're testing for 12 hours or whatever.

300
00:28:53,080 --> 00:29:00,310
And but they're still fresh in your head and it's probably code that you changed yesterday, so it's still high productivity to work on those problems.

301
00:29:00,320 --> 00:29:09,790
The other way of using it, which is much more similar to the way that we used it at FoundationDB, well, I guess we actually did both there.

