321
00:30:49,910 --> 00:30:52,630
You brought up correlation with the code base earlier. Tell me more.

322
00:30:52,630 --> 00:30:55,270
At what level is the correlation?

323
00:30:55,270 --> 00:30:57,150
Is it at the compiled binary level?

324
00:30:57,150 --> 00:31:00,030
Is it at actual source code level based on coverage? How does that work?

325
00:31:00,040 --> 00:31:03,830
Right.

326
00:31:03,830 --> 00:31:09,630
So, basically, we have very good integration with a large number of different language toolchains.

327
00:31:09,630 --> 00:31:13,070
So, it works a little differently depending on the language.

328
00:31:13,070 --> 00:31:19,510
Some of the languages we actually just come in and like transform your binary afterwards, and you don't have to do anything at all.

329
00:31:19,510 --> 00:31:21,750
Like, we do that for Java.

330
00:31:21,750 --> 00:31:25,110
Other languages, like, we give you some flags that you pass to your compiler.

331
00:31:25,110 --> 00:31:27,830
Like, for instance, we do this for C++ and Rust.

332
00:31:27,830 --> 00:31:35,630
And the result in either case is is the same binary that you would run in production,

333
00:31:35,630 --> 00:31:41,750
except it tells us when each line of code gets reached, right?

334
00:31:41,750 --> 00:31:44,750
So far, this is very similar for with instrumenting for code coverage or something like that.

335
00:31:44,760 --> 00:31:50,350
But, the thing is the key is we're not just going to use this for reporting coverage to you.

336
00:31:50,350 --> 00:31:58,270
We're also going to use this for this kind of correlation stuff, for saying, "Oh, we're in like code block A.

337
00:31:58,270 --> 00:32:10,150
We've never tried pausing a thread while we're in code block A before. Let's do that now." Or like, "Oh, this microservice is running function X, and simultaneously this other one over here is also running function X.

338
00:32:10,150 --> 00:32:17,870
Let's interrupt the network connection between them right now and see what happens, right?" And we're going to do that very much in parallel.

339
00:32:17,870 --> 00:32:30,390
And then, the fact that the hypervisor lets us cheaply snapshot and restore stuff means that we can go back to situations that we found interesting and try exploring more from that point.

340
00:32:30,390 --> 00:32:40,510
And that basically lets us amplify the probability of very, very rare events to find really, really hard to discover bugs.

341
00:32:40,510 --> 00:32:44,910
I'll give you a concrete example because that was a little bit vague.

342
00:32:44,910 --> 00:32:51,110
Suppose you have a bug and in order for this bug to happen you need to be in the middle of a transaction rollback

343
00:32:51,120 --> 00:33:00,790
and there needs to be some network problem and right at that moment something has to trigger a leader election in your cluster or something.

344
00:33:00,800 --> 00:33:09,870
If each of those events has a one in 1,000 chance of happening at any given moment, that's like a one in a billion bug, right?

345
00:33:09,870 --> 00:33:11,550
That's like very, very hard for a test to find normally.

346
00:33:11,560 --> 00:33:17,950
But if you're running it in production and you're running on thousands of nodes and whatever, like your customers will totally hit that bug.

347
00:33:17,950 --> 00:33:24,350
So this is like a classic example of a bug that normal testing would never, ever find and that would cause you to get in a page.

348
00:33:24,350 --> 00:33:35,150
And so, but what we can do because we can see what code is running is when we hit that one in 1,000 transaction rollback, we're going to say, "Aha, we made a transaction rollback.

349
00:33:35,150 --> 00:33:40,270
We've never seen that before." Let's do more testing from this point onwards.

350
00:33:40,270 --> 00:33:51,470
And each of the tries we do from that point, if it doesn't hit the next event of interest, we can just reset to the transaction rollback and try more stuff from there, right?

351
00:33:51,470 --> 00:34:01,470
It's like if you're playing a computer game and you can save your game anytime you want it's easier to make progress because you can always just reload your game and and go to the point where things were going well.

352
00:34:01,480 --> 00:34:14,790
And so basically what this means is that for these bugs that require multiple different rare things to happen in sequence, we can find them at a vastly accelerated rate compared to traditional techniques.

353
00:34:14,800 --> 00:34:17,869
You know, I love the example of the saving the game and going back again.

354
00:34:17,869 --> 00:34:21,149
I spent my '90s doing that in all those old games.

355
00:34:21,159 --> 00:34:25,990
Yeah, well, so we actually use computer games a lot for our testing our DST.

356
00:34:25,990 --> 00:34:31,629
So we believe that a good testing system should be able to beat a computer game in the interest of testing it.

357
00:34:31,629 --> 00:34:37,710
And indeed, if you go to our website, we have many videos of Antithesis playing classic computer games and beating them.

358
00:34:37,720 --> 00:34:41,310
I saw the uh Mario example at S Recon last year.

359
00:34:41,320 --> 00:34:43,310
So, back to the example, right?

360
00:34:43,310 --> 00:34:50,389
So, what what you said was when you hit that one in a thousand transaction rollback scenario, you still need that scenario to be hit.

361
00:34:50,399 --> 00:34:59,950
Is there a way you can kind of say like this block of code actually through some sequence of code paths could get hit, and can you force that to happen proactively instead of waiting for it?

362
00:34:59,960 --> 00:35:03,630
So, that's a great question.

363
00:35:03,630 --> 00:35:09,750
We try very hard to make it so that everything gets hit, and there's a number of techniques that we use to do that, right?

364
00:35:09,750 --> 00:35:19,430
One is a very, I think very underused technique called swarm testing, which is applicable to people who are not even doing DST.

365
00:35:19,430 --> 00:35:21,391
Just everybody needs to know about this technique. Okay.

366
00:35:21,400 --> 00:35:30,670
And so, the the the basic idea of swarm testing is you sometimes should run all of your tests with certain features of your program turned off.

367
00:35:30,680 --> 00:35:32,710
That sounds counterintuitive, right?

368
00:35:32,710 --> 00:35:35,110
Why would you want to do that?

369
00:35:35,110 --> 00:35:43,350
Well, imagine a situation where, let's say you have some data structure, and you have two randomized operations that you can do on the data structure.

370
00:35:43,360 --> 00:35:47,270
One of them adds an element to it, and one takes an element out, right?

371
00:35:47,270 --> 00:35:53,350
If both of these things happen with 50% probability, the contents of the data structure will never get very large.

372
00:35:53,360 --> 00:36:02,550
You would require like, let's say you want to get a hundred things in the data structure and then like some garbage collection routine or some compaction happens.

373
00:36:02,550 --> 00:36:07,150
That would require a one in two to the one hundred chance to happen if you're randomly adding and removing things.

374
00:36:07,160 --> 00:36:18,350
But, if we just temporarily disable the remove function, then you're guaranteed to get a whole bunch of adds in a row, and then you're going to trigger that compaction routine, right?

375
00:36:18,350 --> 00:36:28,670
And so, this is like a very motivating example, and it it's like you want to do this with everything, including with your fault injection, right?

376
00:36:28,670 --> 00:36:35,150
One of the most common mistakes that in the early days, before we did this automatically, the customers would make is they would say,

377
00:36:35,150 --> 00:36:45,750
"Ah, I want my code to be maximum powerful, so I want to turn on every single fault." And the problem is there are some faults that will hide bugs, right?

378
00:36:45,750 --> 00:36:53,070
Like imagine there's a bug where your program gets into some really bad state, but if you restart the program, the bug goes away.

379
00:36:53,080 --> 00:36:58,710
Well, now if we are doing a fault that occasionally kills nodes, that can hide that bug, right?

380
00:36:58,710 --> 00:37:06,070
And so what you actually want to do is sometimes run with killing nodes completely turned off, because that might save you from some bug.

381
00:37:06,080 --> 00:37:15,790
And so it's it's exactly the same with everything else and with what kinds of requests you generate and every other kind of behavior that you want to provoke in the system.

382
00:37:15,790 --> 00:37:19,870
Now, it's still possible to get this wrong, it's still possible to overlook things.

383
00:37:19,880 --> 00:37:27,030
And so that's why we come back to like the the sometimes assertions, the logging, that kind of stuff.

384
00:37:27,030 --> 00:37:36,470
If you know the transaction rollbacks are an important situation, you should be adding, you know, a sometimes assertion or a log to your rollback function,

385
00:37:36,470 --> 00:37:44,350
and then we should be looking at every test run and saying, "Did we make rollbacks happen?" And if not, that should be a red test failure, right?

386
00:37:44,350 --> 00:37:45,910
It It could mean that your program has changed.

387
00:37:45,920 --> 00:37:50,670
Maybe you accidentally disabled the rollback function, and that's, you know, now we found a real bug.

388
00:37:50,670 --> 00:37:53,230
But it could also mean that the tests are weak, right?

389
00:37:53,230 --> 00:37:59,350
It could mean that for whatever reason we're not making transactions rollback enough, and that could be very, very important information.

390
00:37:59,360 --> 00:38:01,791
Mhm. Mhm.

391
00:38:01,791 --> 00:38:08,350
So, in that example of the transaction rollback, you know, the other trigger event was a leader election, right?

392
00:38:08,350 --> 00:38:14,070
So, again, how do you draw the correlation of what's happening at the lowest bits and bytes level to at the more application level?

393
00:38:14,080 --> 00:38:17,110
How do you say that, hey, that actually is a leader election?

394
00:38:17,120 --> 00:38:19,390
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

395
00:38:19,390 --> 00:38:26,630
So, the easiest way to do this actually, so the way that requires the least investment from a user is you just look at lines of code.

396
00:38:26,640 --> 00:38:29,510
Every line of code in your program has a purpose.

397
00:38:29,520 --> 00:38:32,311
Some of the lines of code are very boring. They run all the time, right?

398
00:38:32,320 --> 00:38:34,750
The code that like logs a string.

399
00:38:34,750 --> 00:38:38,390
That probably is running all the time in your program.

400
00:38:38,390 --> 00:38:43,110
And so we are going to see it running all the time and we are quickly going to get bored of it.

401
00:38:43,120 --> 00:38:45,030
Across all the programs.

402
00:38:45,030 --> 00:38:47,710
Because the analytics will be an external event or external service.

403
00:38:47,720 --> 00:38:50,191
Totally. Totally true.

404
00:38:50,191 --> 00:38:58,230
But anything that runs rarely, that runs only sometimes, or that runs in very specific conditions, or that runs You could also have,

405
00:38:58,230 --> 00:39:07,350
for example, a line of code that very commonly runs together with some other line of code, but very, very rarely runs by itself.

406
00:39:07,350 --> 00:39:08,910
That could be something very interesting.

407
00:39:08,910 --> 00:39:20,950
So you're not just looking at which lines of code run or don't run at all, but you're actually also looking at the correlations between them and when they run together with each other or not and and what orders they run and so on.

408
00:39:20,950 --> 00:39:31,470
And this is a kind of analysis that human beings are very bad at doing, but it's a kind of analysis that AI is actually very good at doing, machine learning and so on.

409
00:39:31,470 --> 00:39:40,190
And so you can sort of look at the history of what the software has done and then ask, is this novel?

410
00:39:40,190 --> 00:39:42,070
Like, is this interesting?

411
00:39:42,070 --> 00:39:46,230
Is this something This is very similar to what I've seen before or is it very different from what I've seen before?

412
00:39:46,230 --> 00:39:49,070
And then you can use that as an input into where you want to continue testing.

413
00:39:49,080 --> 00:39:52,830
Mhm. So are you using AI ML here to do the correlation?

414
00:39:52,840 --> 00:39:55,991
Yeah. Yeah, yeah.

415
00:39:55,991 --> 00:39:58,590
We actually find It's It's funny.

