00:42:35,320 --> 00:42:41,910
I want to contrast it with one I don't want to call it technique, but one different idea.

443
00:42:41,910 --> 00:42:53,710
If, let's say, programmers or libraries or developers could annotate or or inject annotations in their code base, which actually explicitly was able to assign error probabilities.

444
00:42:53,720 --> 00:42:58,470
How compatible would that be with what you're doing and how much would that alleviate that initial pain?

445
00:42:58,480 --> 00:43:00,430
I think that's a very interesting idea.

446
00:43:00,440 --> 00:43:06,550
So, one thing that we actually do encourage our customers to do.

447
00:43:06,550 --> 00:43:14,030
If you're If you're thinking about annotating, one thing that I think is a very, very powerful technique is something called bugification.

448
00:43:14,040 --> 00:43:24,790
And the idea of bugification is suppose you have some code which usually does one thing, but very rarely could do something else, right?

449
00:43:24,790 --> 00:43:28,150
For example, it could be like writing to a disk, right?

450
00:43:28,150 --> 00:43:40,910
Usually it writes all the data to the disk, but you know, as every systems programmer has learned the hard way, sometimes it writes some of the data to the disk and gives you a special return code that says, "Hey, I only wrote some of the data." You know, by the way, net- networks work the same way, right?

451
00:43:40,920 --> 00:43:44,150
Well, well, usually flush and fsync are actually not on by default, so.

452
00:43:44,160 --> 00:43:46,151
Well, that's true, too. Yeah.

453
00:43:46,151 --> 00:43:54,310
Right, so Right, anytime you have something like this that usually does one thing, but very rarely does something else, if it's actually great,

454
00:43:54,310 --> 00:44:03,670
if you're the one maintaining that code, to basically add a little macro around it or add a little add a little thing, annotation, which is,

455
00:44:03,670 --> 00:44:07,550
"Hey, if I'm running in test, I'm going to do the rare thing on purpose sometimes.

456
00:44:07,550 --> 00:44:13,590
I'm going to roll a random number, and maybe 10% of the time I'm just not going to write all the data.

457
00:44:13,590 --> 00:44:18,430
Or like 10% of the time this thing is going to throw an error, even though it doesn't have to.

458
00:44:18,430 --> 00:44:20,550
Just to test what happens on the other side.

459
00:44:20,550 --> 00:44:22,430
Like is somebody catching that error correctly?

460
00:44:22,440 --> 00:44:26,710
Is somebody handling that return value correctly?

461
00:44:26,710 --> 00:44:29,070
That can drastically accelerate bug discovery, as well.

462
00:44:29,080 --> 00:44:30,631
