Now, obviously, I'm guessing if I was a a front-end developer, I may not be as motivated to get on DST.

570
00:54:20,910 --> 00:54:29,230
But same time, on the other other extreme, if I was a kernel developer or a database vendor or a storage developer, this seems extremely, extremely valuable, right?

571
00:54:29,240 --> 00:54:33,750
In that spectrum, when would be the sweet spot to kind of start considering this?

572
00:54:33,760 --> 00:54:35,870
Right.

573
00:54:35,870 --> 00:54:45,550
So, we've actually started seeing really a lot of traction with application developers, but so far it's specifically in the sector of fintech and finance and blockchain stuff.

574
00:54:45,560 --> 00:54:52,230
They're definitely doing something more abstract, more high-level than a database developer or a kernel or a systems developer.

575
00:54:52,230 --> 00:55:02,110
But the thing is that what they're doing is so important, and it's like so important that it not make mistakes, that that justifies this like much higher level of scrutiny.

576
00:55:02,120 --> 00:55:13,070
So, we recently began working with Ramp, which the corporate card, and they, you know, they have a service that like decides whether authorize charges on the card,

577
00:55:13,070 --> 00:55:20,070
and it's really bad if that like fails to respond to a request or it authorizes it twice or that would be not good.

578
00:55:20,070 --> 00:55:23,390
And so, they really want to test it very, very effectively, and and they've been doing that with us.

579
00:55:23,390 --> 00:55:27,430
We recently also started working with a large hedge fund.

580
00:55:27,430 --> 00:55:31,670
I can't say their name, but it's a big one. And similar kind of situation, right?

581
00:55:31,670 --> 00:55:35,750
Like they're moving hundreds of millions of dollars around.

582
00:55:35,750 --> 00:55:40,750
They really care that that works correctly, and they really care that every operation is is running in the right way.

583
00:55:40,760 --> 00:55:44,270
Looking forward, I think the big buzzword now AI.

584
00:55:44,280 --> 00:55:53,630
With AI writing so much code and if you see in the last few days, it's just it's coding for hours and generating, you know, millions and so much of the code, right?

585
00:55:53,630 --> 00:56:00,590
How do you see, you know, all that all that impacting security bugs, vulnerabilities and uh Sounds Sounds like opportunity.

586
00:56:00,600 --> 00:56:02,270
Yeah.

587
00:56:02,280 --> 00:56:05,230
I think so.

588
00:56:05,230 --> 00:56:07,310
I think basically you know, AI can write a lot of code now.

589
00:56:07,310 --> 00:56:13,390
And the thing is that people are very tempted to just sort of ship it, right?

590
00:56:13,390 --> 00:56:18,950
And to not look too carefully at it, which is not a very good idea if you're in a certain line of work.

591
00:56:18,950 --> 00:56:25,510
And so I think it is a huge opportunity for people trying to develop more sophisticated approaches to software verification, right?

592
00:56:25,510 --> 00:56:29,990
It really highlights writing code is no longer the bottleneck.

593
00:56:29,990 --> 00:56:33,430
Actually, writing code was never the bottleneck, but people thought it was.

594
00:56:33,430 --> 00:56:36,630
But like now writing code is really really really not the bottleneck, right?

595
00:56:36,630 --> 00:56:40,711
The bottleneck is making sure it does the right thing. And that's much harder.

596
00:56:40,720 --> 00:56:43,030
Yeah. I mean, writing is not reading, it is.

597
00:56:43,040 --> 00:56:44,871
Yeah, exactly. Exactly.

598
00:56:44,871 --> 00:56:52,590
Well, that's the other problem with AI, of course, is like it generates a whole bunch of code for you and then as soon as you want to modify anything about it, you're basically starting from scratch.

599
00:56:52,600 --> 00:56:54,750
How you seeing adapting to this?

600
00:56:54,750 --> 00:56:58,030
Like, you know, do you have to change your approach and how you, you know, approach companies?

601
00:56:58,040 --> 00:57:00,510
What's your strategy like now?

602
00:57:00,520 --> 00:57:04,030
It hasn't really affected our approach that much, to be totally honest.

603
00:57:04,030 --> 00:57:11,670
Like, we have a lot of customers who are doing a lot of AI code generation and they're using us to test the results, right?

604
00:57:11,670 --> 00:57:16,030
And I probably that segment of our customers will continue to grow over time.

605
00:57:16,040 --> 00:57:19,750
Nothing about what we're doing has to change that fundamentally, right?

606
00:57:19,750 --> 00:57:22,030
To it to accommodate this use case.

607
00:57:22,040 --> 00:57:25,070
You know, it just means there's more bugs for us to find.

608
00:57:25,080 --> 00:57:30,191
Fair enough. Talking about bugs, you mentioned was it who had a a high bug bounty? I'm sorry, for Ethereum.

609
00:57:30,200 --> 00:57:31,711
That was Ethereum, yeah. Yeah, yeah.

610
00:57:31,720 --> 00:57:38,990
Did you guys qualify for that bug bounty or No, we we since we were a vendor for them, we didn't we didn't qualify for.

611
00:57:38,990 --> 00:57:42,030
I I wish I wish it would have been it would have been a lot of money.

612
00:57:42,040 --> 00:57:51,630
No, we in the early days of the company, we actually thought about using bug bounties as the primary source of revenue, right?

613
00:57:51,630 --> 00:57:53,190
Cuz like some of them get pretty high.

614
00:57:53,200 --> 00:57:59,350
And this thing is actually quite good at finding security bugs, too, that that human beings sometimes miss.

615
00:57:59,360 --> 00:58:03,190
And so, that was certainly a thing we thought about.

616
00:58:03,190 --> 00:58:06,390
In the end, we thought that that was a little bit adversarial.

617
00:58:06,400 --> 00:58:17,590
And that we would rather be embedded with our customer's engineering team and like making them successful and like improving their lives, making it easier for them to do their job.

618
00:58:17,590 --> 00:58:25,910
We thought that in the long term, that would lead to better customer relationships than this like come in from the outside and claim bounties model.

619
00:58:25,920 --> 00:58:32,870
But I I assume somebody's going to be using either DST or AI or something else to claim bug bounties very soon.

620
00:58:32,880 --> 00:58:35,310
Mhm, fair enough.

621
00:58:35,310 --> 00:58:38,630
No, no, I I'd say it's a exciting kind of future out there.

622
00:58:38,630 --> 00:58:48,070
As we wrap up in closing thoughts, what advice would you give to our listeners about improving their own testing practices or evolving their, you know, platforms to get ready for DST?

623
00:58:48,080 --> 00:58:50,790
Right.

624
00:58:50,790 --> 00:58:54,470
It's really The thing I said towards the start of this is I think the single most important piece of advice.

625
00:58:54,480 --> 00:59:01,230
Anytime you see a bug in production, any single time, it is two bugs.

626
00:59:01,240 --> 00:59:02,750
It is two bugs.

627
00:59:02,750 --> 00:59:04,990
It is a bug in your code, but it's also a bug in your tests.

628
00:59:05,000 --> 00:59:09,750
And you should demand more from your tests.

629
00:59:09,750 --> 00:59:13,990
You should cultivate the attitude that it is unacceptable for things to go wrong in production.

630
00:59:14,000 --> 00:59:22,350
And that if a thing does go wrong in production, you should be asking yourself, what could we have done differently that would mean that we found this ahead of time?

631
00:59:22,360 --> 00:59:24,150
Awesome.

632
00:59:24,150 --> 00:59:27,110
For all those who want to learn more, how How they do so and where?

633
